1. What is C# and why is it called a modern object-oriented language?
Definition:  
C# is a strongly typed, object-oriented programming language developed by Microsoft as part of the .NET framework. It combines the power of C++ with the simplicity of Java.

Example:

csharp
class HelloWorld {
    static void Main() {
        Console.WriteLine("Hello, C#!");
    }
}
This shows C#’s simplicity and managed environment.

2. Explain the difference between value types and reference types.
Definition:

Value types store data directly (stack memory).

Reference types store a pointer to the data (heap memory).

Example:

csharp
int x = 10; // value type
string name = "Manoranjan"; // reference type
3. What are classes and objects in C#?
Definition:

Class: Blueprint that defines properties and methods.

Object: Instance of a class.

Example:

csharp
class Car {
    public string Brand;
    public void Drive() {
        Console.WriteLine("Car is driving...");
    }
}

Car myCar = new Car();
myCar.Brand = "Tesla";
myCar.Drive();
4. What is Encapsulation in C#?
Definition:  
Encapsulation is the process of hiding implementation details and exposing only necessary functionality.

Example:

csharp
class BankAccount {
    private double balance = 0;
    public void Deposit(double amount) {
        balance += amount;
    }
    public double GetBalance() {
        return balance;
    }
}
5. Explain Inheritance in C#.
Definition:  
Inheritance allows a class to derive properties and methods from another class.

Example:

csharp
class Animal {
    public void Eat() => Console.WriteLine("Eating...");
}
class Dog : Animal {
    public void Bark() => Console.WriteLine("Barking...");
}
Dog d = new Dog();
d.Eat(); d.Bark();
6. What is Polymorphism in C#?
Definition:  
Polymorphism means “many forms.” It allows methods to behave differently based on context.

Example:

csharp
class Shape {
    public virtual void Draw() => Console.WriteLine("Drawing shape");
}
class Circle : Shape {
    public override void Draw() => Console.WriteLine("Drawing circle");
}
Shape s = new Circle();
s.Draw(); // Output: Drawing circle
7. Difference between abstract class and interface.
Definition:

Abstract class: Can have implementation + abstract methods.

Interface: Only method signatures, no implementation.

Example:

csharp
abstract class Animal {
    public abstract void Sound();
}
interface IWalk {
    void Walk();
}
8. What is sealed class?
Definition:  
A sealed class cannot be inherited.

Example:

csharp
sealed class FinalClass { }
9. Explain static keyword in C#.
Definition:  
Static members belong to the class, not the instance.

Example:

csharp
class MathUtil {
    public static double Pi = 3.14;
}
Console.WriteLine(MathUtil.Pi);
10. What is constructor in C#?
Definition:  
A constructor initializes objects when created.

Example:

csharp
class Student {
    public string Name;
    public Student(string name) {
        Name = name;
    }
}
Student s = new Student("Manoranjan");
11. Types of constructors in C#.
Default

Parameterized

Copy

Static

Example:

csharp
class Demo {
    public Demo() { } // default
    public Demo(int x) { } // parameterized
    public Demo(Demo d) { } // copy
    static Demo() { } // static
}
12. What is method overloading?
Definition:  
Same method name, different parameters.

Example:

csharp
class Calculator {
    public int Add(int a, int b) => a+b;
    public double Add(double a, double b) => a+b;
}
13. What is method overriding?
Definition:  
Child class redefines parent’s method.

Example:

csharp
class Base {
    public virtual void Show() => Console.WriteLine("Base");
}
class Derived : Base {
    public override void Show() => Console.WriteLine("Derived");
}
14. Explain delegate in C#.
Definition:  
Delegate is a type-safe function pointer.

Example:

csharp
delegate void Print(string msg);
Print p = Console.WriteLine;
p("Hello Delegate");
15. What is event in C#?
Definition:  
Events are based on delegates, used for notifications.

Example:

csharp
class Alarm {
    public event Action Ring;
    public void Trigger() => Ring?.Invoke();
}
16. What is LINQ in C#?
Definition:  
Language Integrated Query allows querying collections.

Example:

csharp
var nums = new int[]{1,2,3,4};
var evens = from n in nums where n%2==0 select n;
17. Explain async and await.
Definition:  
Used for asynchronous programming.

Example:

csharp
async Task<int> GetData() {
    await Task.Delay(1000);
    return 42;
}
18. What is boxing and unboxing?
Definition:

Boxing: Converting value type → object.

Unboxing: Object → value type.

Example:

csharp
int x = 10;
object obj = x; // boxing
int y = (int)obj; // unboxing
19. Explain nullable types.
Definition:  
Allows value types to hold null.

Example:

csharp
int? age = null;
20. What is Garbage Collection in C#?
Definition:  
Automatic memory management that frees unused objects.

21. Explain Indexers.
Definition:  
Indexers allow objects to be indexed like arrays.

Example:

csharp
class Sample {
    private string[] data = new string[3];
    public string this[int i] {
        get => data[i];
        set => data[i] = value;
    }
}
22. What is Dependency Injection in C#?
Definition:  
Design pattern to inject dependencies instead of creating them inside a class.

23. Explain Threading in C#.
Definition:  
Threading allows parallel execution.

Example:

csharp
Thread t = new Thread(()=>Console.WriteLine("Running..."));
t.Start();
24. What is Exception Handling in C#?
Definition:  
Mechanism to handle runtime errors.

Example:

csharp
try {
    int x = 10/0;
} catch(Exception ex) {
    Console.WriteLine(ex.Message);
}
25. Explain Properties in C#.
Definition:  
Properties provide controlled access to fields.

Example:

csharp
class Person {
    private string name;
    public string Name {
        get => name;
        set => name = value;
    }
}
